QSS-M chat autocomplete word data
=================================

chat_autocomplete_words.inc is generated by Misc/chat_autocomplete/build_chat_autocomplete.py
from two vendored inputs:

  Misc/chat_autocomplete/chat_autocomplete_english.txt  general-English word frequencies
  Misc/chat_autocomplete/chat_autocomplete_curated.txt  Quake and conversational vocabulary

Both are checked in, so the table regenerates offline and deterministically
with no corpus to download and no network step:

  python3 Misc/chat_autocomplete/build_chat_autocomplete.py

chat_autocomplete_curated.txt is original to QSS-M and carries no third-party
licence obligation.


Provenance of chat_autocomplete_english.txt
-------------------------------------------

Derived from TextBlob's en/en-spelling.txt, filtered to lowercase ASCII words
of 3-24 characters and pruned to entries at or above the runtime confidence
floor (frequency 20), which is everything the engine can ever surface.

TextBlob's en-spelling.txt states that it is based on several public-domain
Project Gutenberg books plus frequency lists from Wiktionary and the British
National Corpus, via Peter Norvig's big.txt corpus.

Note that this makes it a 19th-century literary corpus, not a conversational
one: it underweights ordinary chat vocabulary and overweights period prose and
proper nouns.  chat_autocomplete_curated.txt exists to correct for that, and is
where new words should normally be added.

